rcq("R08-A-08-2", "Structure / function", "Medium",
    "The example of the traveller passing from village to village in paragraph 2 serves to",
    "show that intelligibility gives no principled point at which to divide a continuous chain of varieties.",
    [("show that people living in villages understand neighbouring varieties better than city dwellers do.",
      "The passage contrasts neighbouring villages with the two ends of the chain; it says nothing about city dwellers."),
     ("suggest that the borders of states were first drawn to follow the boundaries between languages.",
      "Paragraph 2 says map boundaries 'tend to coincide' with state borders, but makes no claim about which came first."),
     ("prove that every variety along such a chain must be treated as a separate language in its own right.",
      "The author draws no such conclusion; the example shows that intelligibility 'alone gives no answer', not that there are many languages.")],
    "The example illustrates the claim that intelligibility 'is not transitive' and is followed by the question 'Where on such a chain does one language stop and the next begin?' and the answer that 'Intelligibility alone gives no answer'.",
    "Reversing the relation between state borders and language boundaries.",
    "The function is set by the claim before the example and the question after it.",
    cognitive="Understanding")

rcq("R08-A-08-3", "Inference", "Medium",
    "It can be inferred from the passage that a variety now commonly called a dialect",
    "could come to be called a language by gaining a written standard and a place in schooling, its grammar unchanged.",
    [("is usually harder for outsiders to understand than a variety that is commonly called a language.",
      "Paragraph 3 notes Chinese varieties called dialects that are mutually unintelligible and Scandinavian languages that are intelligible; the label does not track difficulty."),
     ("has a less fully developed grammar than the standard variety from which it is distinguished.",
      "Paragraph 4 says these are 'facts about the social life of a variety, not about its grammar', so no grammatical inferiority is implied."),
     ("will in most cases die out within a few generations unless its speakers succeed in having it recognised as a separate language.",
      "Paragraph 4 says institutions affect 'whether it survives', but the passage does not predict that most dialects will die out.")],
    "Paragraph 3 says a dialect is 'very often simply a variety that lacks the institutions which would have entitled it to be called a language', and paragraph 5 says recognition requires 'a change in those arrangements' rather than in 'the linguistic facts'.",
    "Moving from the point that institutions affect survival to a prediction that most dialects will die.",
    "Requires combining the definition in paragraph 3 with the conclusion in paragraph 5; one distractor overstates a real point in paragraph 4.",
    cognitive="Analysis")

rcq("R08-A-08-4", "Author's tone / attitude", "Difficult",
    "The author's attitude towards the writers who conclude that the distinction is 'empty' is best described as",
    "accepting the observation they start from while firmly rejecting the conclusion they draw from it.",
    [("in full agreement, since the author also regards the distinction as politics dressed up as science.",
      "The author answers their conclusion with 'It does not follow' and argues that institutional boundaries are real."),
     ("dismissive of both their starting point and their conclusion, which the author thinks rest on bad evidence.",
      "The author shares their starting point: paragraphs 2 and 3 argue that the boundaries are drawn by institutions, not by sound systems."),
     ("uncertain, since the author thinks that the question of whether the distinction is real cannot yet be settled.",
      "The author is not undecided: paragraph 4 states that institutional facts 'are facts' and paragraph 5 says what the distinction is.")],
    "The writers' premise, that the line is drawn by institutions, is the author's own conclusion from paragraphs 2 and 3. The author rejects only the inference to emptiness: 'It does not follow', because a boundary 'drawn by institutions rather than by sound systems' is not thereby 'unreal'.",
    "Missing that the author agrees with the premise, and so choosing outright dismissal.",
    "The attitude has two parts that point in opposite directions, and two distractors each capture only one of them.",
    cognitive="Analysis", exams=HARD)

rcq("R08-A-08-5", "Application / analogy", "Difficult",
    "A campaign argues that a regional variety deserves recognition as a language because linguists have measured large differences between its grammar and that of the standard. The author would most likely respond that the campaign",
    "rests its case on the wrong ground, since recognition turns on a society's arrangements rather than on measured features of speech.",
    [("is well founded, since measured grammatical distance is the most objective basis there is for applying the label.",
      "Paragraph 5 says the difference 'is not a property of the speech itself, waiting to be measured', which rules out this response."),
     ("is illegitimate, since the line between a language and a dialect is in the end nothing more than politics.",
      "Paragraph 4 rejects the view that the distinction is 'empty', and paragraph 5 says such requests 'should be judged on those terms', not dismissed."),
     ("should first establish whether speakers of the standard can understand the variety without any difficulty.",
      "Paragraph 2 argues that intelligibility 'does not deliver what it promises', so the author would not propose it as the decisive test.")],
    "Paragraph 5 says a community seeking recognition 'is asking not for a correction of the linguistic facts but for a change in those arrangements'. A campaign that argues from grammatical measurements treats the question as one of linguistic fact, which is where the author says the mistake lies.",
    "Choosing the response that calls the campaign illegitimate, which treats the author as the sceptic of paragraph 4 whom the author answers.",
    "Each distractor applies one of the positions the passage examines, so the solver must apply the author's final view precisely.",
    cognitive="Application", exams=HARD)
